findings.body:
The propagated command has seven elements (HSETEX, key, KEEPTTL, FIELDS, 1, field, value), but the call passes `6` as argc. `rewriteClientCommandVector` reads only `argc` varargs, so the value is silently dropped. The replica then receives `HSETEX h1 KEEPTTL FIELDS 1 f1` with no value, which is invalid and will error on the replica or in AOF replay. The new test's stream assertion (`{hsetex h1 KEEPTTL FIELDS 1 f1 *}`) would fail for the same reason, so the test was evidently not run against this diff.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2565-2573"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The fix is a one-character change: argc should be 7.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2571-2572"}
rewriteClientCommandVector(c, 7, shared.hsetex, c->argv[1], shared.keepttl,
                    shared.fields, shared.integers[1], c->argv[2], newobj);

The local `hashTypeSet` call in `hincrbyfloatCommand` used to pass `HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE | HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL`. The PR drops `HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL`, which was meant only for the replicated command. Without the flag, `hashTypeSet` removes the field's expiry: the listpack branch calls `listpackExUpdateExpiry(..., HASH_LP_NO_TTL)` (t_hash.c:959-964) and the hashtable branch calls `hfieldPersist` (t_hash.c:989-992). So every HINCRBYFLOAT on a field with a TTL now strips that TTL on the master itself. The master and any replica that receives `HSETEX KEEPTTL` will then diverge, which is the exact bug this PR claims to fix. The original KEEP_TTL flag was correct and should be restored.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2559-2559"}

::callout{type="security"}
Data-integrity regression: a field that had a TTL loses it on the master after a plain `HINCRBYFLOAT`, with no error and no log.
